<circuit version="1.1.0-SR2" rev="261114" stepSize="1000000" stepsPS="1000000" NLsteps="100000" reaStep="1000000" animate="0" noise="1" noiseBW="1000000" noiseScale="1" noiseSeed="0" noiseStart="0" noiseStop="0" noiseOff="R2" >
<description>
# Transient noise on a scope

Shows SimulIDE's transient noise (docs/NOISE.md): the thermal noise of a 1 MΩ
resistor on a 10 pF node, amplified 1000 times so it is plain on the scope.

- **SIG**: 1 kHz sine, 50 µV peak, into two identical paths.
- **R1 1 MΩ, C1 10 pF → U1** (op amp open loop, gain 1000, ±5 V): yellow trace.
  The node's noise is √(kT/C) = 20.4 µV rms, so the 50 mV output sine carries
  about 20 mV rms of noise (band limited by R1·C1, 16 kHz).
- **R2 1 MΩ, C2 10 pF → U2**: blue trace, the same path with R2 in `noiseOff`:
  a clean reference sine.
- The op amps are behavioural and make no noise themselves.

Saved with noise on (whole run, bandwidth 1 MHz, scale ×1, random seed,
noiseOff R2). The noise button in the toolbar switches it off and on (a running
simulation restarts): the yellow trace becomes as clean as the blue one.

Simulated [SIMULATED, noise ×1, 1 MHz, 20 ms]: U1 output 40.8 mV rms, U2 35.2 mV
rms (the sine alone, 50 mV/√2): the noise part is √(40.8² − 35.2²) = 20.6 mV,
i.e. 20.6 µV at the node against 20.4 µV for kT/C (1 %).

Try: `noiseScale` 0.1 or 10; a larger C1 (100 pF: √10 less noise); `noiseBW`
does not change it (the RC sets the bandwidth). Noise costs speed: every node
is solved every 0.5 µs at 1 MHz.

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.4.1 (commit b897f9d)
- Circuit: noise_scope_demo.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

None reported.
</description>


